Findings, each with what it rests on:

1. **Mild conditions cost little.** At severity 1, mAP@0.5 falls by between {{p2/test/rain_s1/all/map50_delta|abs}} (rain) and {{p2/test/motion_blur_s1/all/map50_delta|abs}} (motion blur). This small loss is a finding in its own right.
2. **Severe conditions cost a lot, haze excepted.** At severity 3 mAP@0.5 falls to between {{p2/test/low_light_s3/all/map50}} (low light) and {{p2/test/rain_s3/all/map50}} (rain); haze only reaches {{p2/test/haze_s3/all/map50}}.
3. **Blur, JPEG, rain and downscaling fail silently.** Violator flags almost disappear (Table 2) while micro precision stays between {{p2/test/jpeg_s3/all/precision}} and {{p2/test/rain_s3/all/precision}}. Alert precision therefore cannot reveal the drop; a monitor has to see the missing boxes.
4. **Low light fails loudly instead.** Positive PPE classes collapse (helmet recall {{p2/test/low_light_s3/helmet/recall}} against {{p2/test/clean/helmet/recall}} clean) but no_helmet recall does not fall, and the false-alert share rises to {{p2/test/low_light_s3/person_any_violation/false_alert_rate}}, with {{p2/test/low_light_s3/person_any_violation/nonviolator_flag_rate}} of labelled non-violators flagged (none clean). Dark images push the model towards "no PPE".
5. **no_goggle is the most fragile violation class.** Its recall falls in all 18 cells; already at JPEG severity 2 the drop is {{p2/test/jpeg_s2/no_goggle/recall_delta|signed}} (n = {{p2/test/jpeg_s2/no_goggle/recall_delta|n}}), and at severity 3 it reaches zero under four of six conditions (Table 3).
6. **Person recall is the most robust class** except at the severe end of motion blur ({{p2/test/motion_blur_s3/person/recall_delta|signed}}), low light ({{p2/test/low_light_s3/person/recall_delta|signed}}), JPEG ({{p2/test/jpeg_s3/person/recall_delta|signed}}) and rain ({{p2/test/rain_s3/person/recall_delta|signed}}).

Not comparable: no_boots starts at zero, so no drop can be observed for it (floor). Downscaling changes geometry and adds a grey border that training
images never had. Low light and haze also alter the black letterbox bars that many test images carry. Person-level rows rest on
{{p2/test/clean/person_any_violation/violation_recall|n}} labelled violators, so one violator moves them by about two hundredths.
